## 🆘 Troubleshooting

- **Forgot feature branch:** `git checkout -b feature/my-work` from current state
- **Update from develop:** `git checkout feature/my-work && git merge develop`
- **Accidentally committed to develop:** `git reset --soft HEAD~1`, then create feature branch and recommit
- **PR conflicts:** merge develop into your feature branch, resolve conflicts, push
- **Bot review not detected:** Check directly, filtered by the SPECIFIC bot's login (never `.user.type == "Bot"` — both gate bots are Apps, so that filter matches either one and can resolve to the wrong gate) and WITH `--paginate` (the endpoint pages at 30; without it, a bot's latest review on a long-running PR can sit past page 1 and look "not detected" when it was actually just never fetched):
  ```bash
  gh api --paginate repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls/<N>/reviews --jq '.[] | select(.user.login == "ds9-tester-bot[bot]")'
  gh api --paginate repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls/<N>/reviews --jq '.[] | select(.user.login == "ai-security-review-bot[bot]")'
  ```
- **`reviewDecision` always shows `REVIEW_REQUIRED`:** This is expected — GitHub Team plan doesn't count bot reviews. Always check the reviews list directly (per-login, as above), never `reviewDecision`.
- **A rebase or force-push re-points an EXISTING review's `commit_id` onto the new head** — head-SHA keying alone does not survive this. On an ordinary push (no rebase), a stale review keeps its old `commit_id`, so filtering on `commit_id == <current HEAD>` correctly excludes it. On a rebase, GitHub re-associates whatever reviews were current at rebase time onto the rebased head, so that same filter can match a review that never saw the rebased content — an "APPROVED" that looks fresh by SHA but predates the content it's supposedly approving. **Say precisely what is and isn't mechanically closed here — the two layers have different guarantees:**
  - **Mechanically closed** for a rewrite that happens *while the loop is actively polling*: each poll diffs the new head against the previous poll's own head via `compare/PREV...NEW`. Only `status: "ahead"` (a true fast-forward) is treated as an ordinary push; `diverged`/`behind`/unreadable is treated as a rewrite unconditionally, regardless of any review's `commit_id` or `submitted_at`, and stamps the current wall-clock time as a floor no review can have been submitted before. Verified against this repo's own PR #851 `HeadRefForcePushedEvent` history (`gh api graphql` on the PR's `timelineItems`): the ordinary push in that history compared `ahead` (ahead_by=2, behind_by=0); the actual force-push compared `diverged` (ahead_by=8, behind_by=7) — a real, measured discriminator, not a hypothetical one.
  - **Heuristic only, with a known gap**, for a rewrite that already happened *before the loop started polling* (no previous head to diff against) or when reproducing this by hand outside the loop: falls back to requiring each matching review's `submitted_at` to be strictly after the head commit's own `commit.committer.date`, failing closed (a sentinel date that sorts after everything real) if that date can't be read. This catches an ordinary rebase, where committer dates get rewritten to "now" — but it does **not** catch a force-push that re-points HEAD onto a pre-existing *older* commit (drop the tip commit and force-push, or `rebase --committer-date-is-author-date`): a stale review submitted after that old commit's own original committer date, but before it was force-pushed back into place, still clears this check even though it never saw the content as HEAD. Do not describe this fallback as a guarantee — it is a heuristic that narrows the window, not a proof.

  Reproduce the heuristic layer by hand:
  ```bash
  HEAD_SHA=$(gh pr view <N> --json headRefOid --jq '.headRefOid')
  HEAD_COMMIT_DATE=$(gh api "repos/{owner}/{repo}/commits/$HEAD_SHA" --jq '.commit.committer.date')
  gh api --paginate repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls/<N>/reviews \
    --jq '[.[] | select(.commit_id == "'"$HEAD_SHA"'")] | .[] | "\(.user.login)|\(.submitted_at)|\(.state)"'
  # Compare each submitted_at against $HEAD_COMMIT_DATE by hand: at-or-before it means that
  # review was re-pointed by the rebase, not freshly submitted against this content.
  ```
  As a sanity check that your `commit_id` filter is discriminating at all, a negative control against an all-zero fake SHA must return zero rows:
  ```bash
  FAKE="0000000000000000000000000000000000000000"
  gh api --paginate repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls/<N>/reviews --jq '[.[] | select(.commit_id == "'"$FAKE"'")] | length'   # MUST be 0
  ```
  Even with both layers in place, treat a rebase/force-push like a fresh review round as a matter of course: push, then re-spawn BOTH bots and re-seed both `LAST_*_AT`. The guards stop most stale approvals from silently passing the gate; they don't substitute for getting fresh eyes on the rewritten content, and the heuristic layer's known gap means an operator who skips the re-review is still exposed in that one case.
- **NEVER** store `gh api` JSON in shell variables then pipe through `echo | jq` — control characters break parsing. Always use `gh api --jq` instead.
- **`$?` after a pipe (e.g. `gh-bot-test ... | tail`) reports the PIPE's exit code, not the command's** — a failed submission can silently read as success. Capture the command's own output/exit code before piping it anywhere.
- **Manual merge after bot approval:** Verify APPROVED reviews exist from BOTH `ds9-tester-bot[bot]` AND `ai-security-review-bot[bot]` (per the queries above) and that `kb-sweep <ITEM-ID>` shows no `PROTECTED SUBITEMS UNRESOLVED`, then `gh pr merge <N> --squash --delete-branch --admin && kb-done`
- **`gh pr merge --delete-branch` fails locally from a worktree even though the API merge succeeds** (git refuses to delete a branch checked out in another worktree): the merge itself still went through — verify with `gh pr view <N> --json state`, then clean up the remote branch manually if needed: `git push <remote> --delete <branch>`
- **`gh-bot-review`/`gh-bot-test` errors:** If an alias isn't loaded, call the underlying script directly: `bash ~/.config/gh-review-bot/gh-bot-review.sh --pr <N> --repo <owner>/<repo> --event APPROVE --body "text"` or `bash ~/.config/gh-tester-bot/gh-bot-test.sh --pr <N> --repo <owner>/<repo> --event APPROVE --body "text"`. Both bots require org-specific GitHub App setup (private key + config at `~/.config/gh-review-bot/` and `~/.config/gh-tester-bot/` respectively) that this tap does not provision automatically — see your org's GitHub App setup docs.
- **Either bot command exits 1 with `Could not auto-detect repository`:** you omitted `--repo owner/repo`. Both scripts try to auto-detect it via `git remote get-url origin`, which fails whenever your remote isn't named `origin`.

